ity dates that require careful interpretation.\n\nThe key insight emerges from understanding the question's precise intent: identifying treasury bills issued on those specific dates and determining their current outstanding value as reflected in the January 30, 1953 market data. By cross-referencing the Offerings and Market Quotations tables, I've found two critical data points - a bill from October 3, 1952 valued at $2,502 million, and another from December 18, 1952 worth $1,200 million.\n\nThe slight one-day discrepancy between 12/18 and 12/19 appears negligible, suggesting these represent the same treasury bill issuance. Calculating the absolute difference yields $1,302 million, which rounds to $1,302 million when expressed in whole dollar millions.\n\nI'll verify the precise figure to ensure accuracy in my calculation.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_d86539c820f24a329d0cfa9c","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\n# Based on my analysis:\n# - Treasury bill issued on 10/3/52: $2,502 million outstanding (as of Jan 30, 1953)\n# - Treasury bill issued on 12/18/52: $1,200 million outstanding (as of Jan 30, 1953)\n\n# Calculate the difference\noct_3_amount = 2502\ndec_18_amount = 1200\n\ndifference = abs(oct_3_amount - dec_18_amount)\nprint(f\"October 3, 1952 issue: ${oct_3_amount} million\")\nprint(f\"December 18, 1952 issue: ${dec_18_amount} million\")\nprint(f\"Absolute difference: ${difference} million\")\nprint(f\"Rounded to nearest whole number: {round(difference)}\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
